WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:05.120
Even a few years after the conclusions of
all-time classic shows such as Mad Men, Breaking

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:09.730
Bad, and Parks and Recreation, we're still
in "The Second Golden Age of TV," also known

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:14.089
as "Peak TV," also known as "There is Far
Too Much Good Stuff to Watch on Broadcast

00:00:14.089 --> 00:00:17.230
Television, Cable, and At Least Four Streaming
Services."

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:22.199
There's just so much to watch: Game of Thrones,
Westworld, The Walking Dead, Atlanta, Silicon

00:00:22.199 --> 00:00:23.199
Valley.

00:00:23.199 --> 00:00:27.259
Still, despite all this can't-miss TV, there
are plenty of other series you can go ahead

00:00:27.259 --> 00:00:28.259
and skip.

00:00:28.259 --> 00:00:32.619
The following are our picks for the very worst
shows of 2018… so far.

00:00:32.619 --> 00:00:34.239
Alex, Inc.

00:00:34.239 --> 00:00:39.100
ABC promoted Alex, Inc. as the triumphant
return of Zach Braff, away from TV for almost

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:41.720
a decade after Scrubs finished its nine-season
run.

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:42.720
But it didn't quite work out that way.

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:46.829
This is partly a typical family comedy about
a goofy dad, his long-suffering wife, and

00:00:46.829 --> 00:00:47.829
their kids.

00:00:47.829 --> 00:00:51.510
But it's also about a guy who has a midlife
crisis, starts his own company, and documents

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:53.289
that journey through podcasting.

00:00:53.289 --> 00:00:57.570
Based on the true story of StartUp host Alex
Blumberg, Alex, Inc. reveals to audiences

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:01.539
the not-very-visually-entertaining world of
people talking into microphones.

00:01:01.539 --> 00:01:05.850
While podcasts are mainstream at this point,
the show treats the concept like it's cutting-edge

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:09.580
technology, and people treat Alex like he's
a genius for making one.

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:10.980
Simply unbelievable.

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:13.729
"I hate to disagree with you.

00:01:13.729 --> 00:01:17.460
I hate it enough to make two fists."

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:18.460
Child Support

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:23.200
Ricky Gervais is a TV legend for co-creating
and starring in the original British Office,

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:27.420
as well as the show business satire Extras
and the nursing-home dramedy Derek.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:31.829
But his latest project isn't a single-camera
comedy or nuanced character study.

00:01:31.829 --> 00:01:36.470
Instead, Child Support is one part Kids Say
the Darndest Things, one part overly convoluted

00:01:36.470 --> 00:01:37.470
game show.

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:41.880
In a TV studio, host Fred Savage asks adult
contestants trivia questions.

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:45.560
If they answer correctly, their responses
are compared to the answers given by a panel

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:46.670
of adorable children.

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:50.369
Gervais asks the kids the same questions in
a creepy-looking focus group.

00:01:50.369 --> 00:01:54.430
It's more unusual than amusing, and as The
Laughing Place has noted, the questions are

00:01:54.430 --> 00:01:57.950
"painfully easy" and the show "tries too hard
to be funny."

00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:05.990
"Benjamin Franklin said, 'Early to bed and
early to rise makes a man healthy, wealthy,

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:07.719
and' what?"

00:02:07.719 --> 00:02:08.719
Deception

00:02:08.719 --> 00:02:13.690
The early 2000s are over, but that era's biggest
contribution to television endures: the crime

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:15.640
procedural with a sassy twist.

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:19.930
The likes of Bones, Castle, and The Mentalist
are all gone, but they continue in spirit

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:21.650
with ABC's Deception.

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:25.970
It's about a celebrity magician with the very
celebrity-magician name of "Cameron Black."

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:30.320
After his career is destroyed by a scandal,
he teams up with the FBI to solve crimes.

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:34.480
That's right, he uses his gift for "illusions"
to somehow get into the minds of criminals,

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:36.420
which is a pretty weak premise.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:39.900
But winning over viewers is one magic trick
Cameron has not managed to pull off.

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:43.690
Add in meaningless supporting characters,
and what's been described by Variety as "a

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:47.960
fatal lack of surprises," and there's not
much left to watch.

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:49.300
Life Sentence

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:54.860
The breezy CW dramedy Life Sentence offers
up basically the same setup as the 2001 Disney

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:57.000
movie Max Keeble's Big Move.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:00.460
But in this case, it's about a young woman
named Stella who thought she was dying of

00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:01.460
cancer.

00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:05.090
For years, she lived everyday like it was
her last, only to have her cancer cured, leaving

00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:07.910
her to deal with the ramifications of her
impulsive decisions.

00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:12.490
There's a decent premise here, but the execution
just feels too cruel to its main character,

00:03:12.490 --> 00:03:14.620
while leaving other moments overly sentimental.

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:17.850
Ultimately, it's just an uneven mix.

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:19.410
Living Biblically

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:20.540
Based on humorist A.J.

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:25.280
Jacobs' book The Year of Living Biblically,
this CBS sitcom is about a critic who decides

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:29.170
to live out every last detail of his life
according to the Holy Bible.

00:03:29.170 --> 00:03:33.330
While that's a refillable premise, it's difficult
to make religion funny and palatable to a

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:34.540
broad audience.

00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:38.950
Secular audiences are alienated, while the
show also skirts offending the faithful, never

00:03:38.950 --> 00:03:40.500
really finding an audience.

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:44.540
Ultimately, Living Biblically commits the
cardinal comedy sin of just not being very

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:45.840
funny.

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:46.840
The Resident

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:51.760
Matt Czuchry stars as Dr. Conrad Hawkins,
a medical rogue who plays by his own rules.

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:56.100
Dr. Hawkins makes rash decisions and ignores
directives from his superiors because he has

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:59.260
gut feelings about what crazy medical procedures
to use.

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:02.560
Except he doesn't come across as heroic; just
arrogant and smarmy.

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:06.650
Instead, he's just another antihero who thinks
he can get away with anything like we've seen

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:07.880
so many times before.

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:13.620
And that is The Resident's biggest flaw: it
doesn't offer anything new to the TV landscape.

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:15.200
Sweetbitter

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:19.320
This Starz series follows naive Tess, who
leaves her hometown behind to move to the

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:20.320
Big Apple.

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:24.530
New York is unexpectedly tough, until Tess
is taken under the wing of colleagues at the

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:27.380
fancy restaurant where she finds work almost
immediately.

00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:30.760
She reminds them of themselves, only younger
and more optimistic.

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:35.310
Except cliches are a big problem on Sweetbitter,
a very slow-moving show where nothing much

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:38.540
happens beyond a woman working in a restaurant
and making friends.

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:40.650
Robert Lloyd of The Los Angeles Times called
it:

00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:45.500
"A meal that looks great on the plate but
makes little impression on the tongue."

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:47.210
Splitting Up Together

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:52.290
This ABC family sitcom is at least an innovative,
if not vaguely depressing, twist on an old

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:56.000
format: Mom and Dad are in the process of
divorcing, but rather than have one of them

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:00.090
move out, both parents stay in the house,
keeping separate living quarters so as to

00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:03.650
still provide their kids with an uninterrupted
and stable home life.

00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:07.850
While that's a modern and progressive solution,
it's a hard sell for a silly little sitcom.

00:05:07.850 --> 00:05:09.480
And according to the Orlando Sentinel,

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:14.100
"It feels like a long, forced push to get
[the main couple] back together."

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:15.100
Here and Now

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:19.360
Set in the progressive city of Portland, Oregon,
Here and Now is about a progressive family

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:24.250
made up of a philosophy professor, his therapist
wife, and their four mostly grown children,

00:05:24.250 --> 00:05:25.580
adopted from around the world.

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:30.120
Their one biological child is in high school,
where she encounters many didactic conversations

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:34.070
about social issues, offset by weird dreams
and prophetic hallucinations.

00:05:34.070 --> 00:05:38.610
Basically, imagine if Portlandia wasn't a
comedy, but was instead just as serious and

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:40.420
ominous as The Leftovers.

00:05:40.420 --> 00:05:41.710
According to Joel Keller of Decider,

00:05:41.710 --> 00:05:45.650
"Here and Now is so overwrought that it makes
The Handmaid's Tale look like a light-hearted

00:05:45.650 --> 00:05:46.920
romp by comparison."

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:47.770
Dark.

